{"content":[{"c":["NAOMI"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Wife of Elimelech and the mother of Mahlon and Chilion. A member of Judah’s tribe, she lived in Bethlehem during the period of the judges. Her story is told in the book of Ruth. Because of a severe famine in Canaan, Naomi temporarily resettled with her family in the land of Moab, east of the Dead Sea (",{"href":"?bref=Ruth.1.1-2","r":"Ruth.1.1-2","rawR":"Ruth.1.1-2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ruth.1.1-2","value":"Ruth.1.1-2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ru 1:1-2"},"). Following the death of her husband and two sons in Moab (vv ",{"href":"?bref=Ruth.1.3-5","r":"Ruth.1.3-5","rawR":"Ruth.1.3-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ruth.1.3-5","value":"Ruth.1.3-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"3-5"},"), Naomi returned to Bethlehem with Ruth, her Moabitess daughter-in-law (vv ",{"href":"?bref=Ruth.1.8-22","r":"Ruth.1.8-22","rawR":"Ruth.1.8-22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ruth.1.8-22","value":"Ruth.1.8-22"}],"t":"ref","v":"8-22"},"). Upon meeting her friends, she told them not to call her Naomi, meaning “pleasant,” but Mara, meaning “bitter,” for she said, “I went away full, but the ",{"c":["Lord"],"sourceClass":"divine-name","t":"span"}," has brought me home empty” (vv ",{"href":"?bref=Ruth.1.20-21","r":"Ruth.1.20-21","rawR":"Ruth.1.20-21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ruth.1.20-21","value":"Ruth.1.20-21"}],"t":"ref","v":"20-21"},", ",{"c":["nlt"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"},"). Naomi’s domestic problems were eventually resolved when Ruth married Boaz, Elimelech’s near kin (chs ",{"href":"?bref=Ruth.2.1-4.22","r":"Ruth.2.1-4.22","rawR":"Ruth.2.1-4.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ruth.2.1-4.22","value":"Ruth.2.1-4.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"2–4"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Naomi","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Ru 1:1-2","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Ruth.1.1-2","rawQueryValue":"Ruth.1.1-2","sourceRefExact":"Ruth.1.1-2"},{"anchorText":"3-5","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Ruth.1.3-5","rawQueryValue":"Ruth.1.3-5","sourceRefExact":"Ruth.1.3-5"},{"anchorText":"8-22","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Ruth.1.8-22","rawQueryValue":"Ruth.1.8-22","sourceRefExact":"Ruth.1.8-22"},{"anchorText":"20-21","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Ruth.1.20-21","rawQueryValue":"Ruth.1.20-21","sourceRefExact":"Ruth.1.20-21"},{"anchorText":"2–4","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Ruth.2.1-4.22","rawQueryValue":"Ruth.2.1-4.22","sourceRefExact":"Ruth.2.1-4.22"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"42025fa25bb5f354802bb65bc818a728aeeb0646dbf590387c01c0ccee9c8c16","sourceItemName":"Naomi","sourceItemOrder":43,"sourceMember":"Articles/N.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Naomi","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Naomi","title":"Naomi","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}